Mill System Disk

make_system_disk.py builds "Mill System Disk", an 800K startup floppy with System 6.0.5 and
MacTCP 2.0.6, trimmed to leave room for Mill on a 1 MB Mac Plus. It builds the disk from
Apple's own System 6.0.5 disk set and Apple's System 7.5 Update 2.0, which it downloads, so
the disk is never given out with Mill.

There are two editions:

    #1E6-DM01-N  Network Edition    for LocalTalk or Ethernet. Has AppleShare.
    #1E6-DM01-P  Personal Edition   for a modem. Has MacPPP 2.0.1 in place of AppleShare.

Pick the Network Edition if the Mac is on LocalTalk or Ethernet, and the Personal Edition if
it dials in with a modem. Both are named "Mill System Disk".

    python3 tools/systemdisk/make_system_disk.py OUTDIR [--personal] [--mactcp PATH | --no-mactcp]

It needs Python 3 and an Internet connection, and nothing else (on Debian and Ubuntu,
the python3-venv package too). It writes the Network Edition, or with --personal the
Personal Edition:

    OUTDIR/Mill-System-Disk-Network.image   a Disk Copy 4.2 image, for Disk Copy and most emulators
    OUTDIR/Mill-System-Disk-Network.dsk     the same disk as a raw image
    OUTDIR/Mill-System-Disk-Personal.image  the same for the Personal Edition
    OUTDIR/Mill-System-Disk-Personal.dsk

Mini vMac opens Disk Copy images locked. Use the .dsk there, so that MacTCP can save its
settings.

The first run makes a Python virtual environment in tools/systemdisk/cache, installs pinned
copies of machfs 1.3 and macresources 1.2 into it (both MIT licensed, checked by hash), and
downloads Apple's four 6.0.5 disks and one disk of System 7.5 Update 2.0 (about 3 MB in
all) into the same folder, and MacPPP too for the Personal Edition. Later runs use the cache.
--cache DIR puts the cache somewhere else. Delete the folder to start over.


What it does

1. Downloads SSW_6.0.5_Disk1of4.sea.bin through Disk4of4.sea.bin, and
   Sys_7.5_Update_2.0-03of14.sea.bin, from the Internet Archive's copies of Apple's old
   download site (download.info.apple.com). A file whose SHA-256 is not the one in the
   script is refused.
2. Unpacks each one in Python: MacBinary, then a StuffIt archive, then a Disk Copy 4.2
   image. Every CRC and checksum is checked.
3. Takes MacTCP out of the Installer archive ("System 7.5 Update 2.0 Tome 3") on the
   Update 2.0 disk, and checks it against the SHA-256 in the script.
4. For the Personal Edition, downloads mac-ppp-201.hqx from the Info-Mac archive at FUNET
   (ftp.funet.fi) and checks its SHA-256, then decodes the BinHex and unpacks the StuffIt
   archive in it, checking every CRC, and checks PPP and Config PPP against the SHA-256s in
   the script.
5. Builds the disk from the four 6.0.5 disks (System Tools, Utilities 1, Utilities 2, and
   Printing Tools), MacTCP, and for the Personal Edition MacPPP.


What is on the disk

System Folder, with:
  - System 6.0.5, less the Mac II ROM patch (PTCH 376), the Mac II video driver
    (DRVR 120), and three alert sounds (Clink-Klank, Boing, and Monkey). The Network
    Edition's has AppleShare's drivers, as the Installer adds them.
  - Finder, General, Clipboard File, Keyboard, Mouse, Sound, and Map
  - MacTCP 2.0.6 (see "MacTCP" below)
  - Network Edition: AppleShare. Personal Edition: PPP and Config PPP (see "MacPPP" below).
  - LaserWriter, Laser Prep, and ImageWriter

There is no MultiFinder, and the fonts are the ones System 6.0.5 comes with. The disk starts
up with 6.0.5's own boot blocks.

When made, the Network Edition has 78K free and the Personal Edition 80K. Both have about
52K free after the first startup, once MacTCP DNR and the Finder's Desktop file are made.

--atimagewriter puts AppleTalk ImageWriter on the Network Edition in place of AppleShare.
--all-disks also writes Apple's four 6.0.5 disks to OUTDIR as raw images.


MacTCP

Mill needs MacTCP to reach the network. Apple's System 6.0.5 disks don't include it. In the
1990s MacTCP came with site licenses and Internet starter kits; Apple later included it in
the free System 7.5.3 release, which is where the tool takes it from: System 7.5 Update 2.0,
the free update that brings System 7.5 to 7.5.3, has MacTCP 2.0.6 on its third disk. MacTCP
2.0.6 runs on System 6.0.5.

The Update's Installer keeps MacTCP compressed in an archive (a "tome"). tome.py, beside the
script, unpacks it. It reads only as much of that format as MacTCP needs, so the script checks
the result against a pinned SHA-256.

There is no MacTCP DNR to copy: MacTCP makes it at the first startup. It needs about 20K free
for that; the disk has more than enough.

To use your own copy of MacTCP instead, pass it with --mactcp:

    --mactcp MacTCP.bin       a MacBinary file of the MacTCP control panel
    --mactcp folder           a folder with MacTCP.bin in it (MacTCP DNR.bin too, if you
                              have it)
    --mactcp disk.image       a disk image (raw or Disk Copy 4.2) with MacTCP on it

--no-mactcp leaves MacTCP off the disk. You can add it later: start up from the Mill System
Disk, drag MacTCP into the System Folder, and restart.

Set MacTCP up before using Mill: open the Control Panel from the Apple menu,
click MacTCP, choose LocalTalk or Ethernet (PPP on the Personal Edition), then click More...
and set how the Mac gets its address, the gateway, and a name server (domain "." and the
server's address, marked as the default). Restart for the settings to take effect.


MacPPP

The Personal Edition has MacPPP 2.0.1, from Merit Network and the University of Michigan:
PPP, which MacTCP lists as a way to connect, and the Config PPP control panel, where you set
up the modem and the number to dial. Together they take about 48K on the disk.

The tool downloads it from the Info-Mac archive's copy at FUNET:

    ftp://ftp.funet.fi/pub/mac/info-mac/comm/inet/conn/ppp/mac-ppp-201.hqx

and puts both files on the disk as they are. Its license, from its documentation:

    MacPPP was developed at, and is copyrighted by, the Merit Network, Inc. and the
    University of Michigan. Merit and the University of Michigan grant an unlimited license
    for use and redistribution of the executable program provided that it is not sold for
    profit, either as is or as part of another product. Charges to recover the cost of
    duplication and distribution are permitted. MacPPP is offered "as is"-neither Merit nor
    the University of Michigan make any guarantees about the performance or reliability of
    the software.

Config PPP reserves 128K of system heap at startup. On a 1 MB Plus that leaves Mill about
30K less memory than the Network Edition does.

Mill opens MacTCP only when it needs the network, so it doesn't dial when it starts, unless
the connection check is turned on from the Settings page.


